<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anabaptism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">16th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Anabaptists</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anabaptism is a radical Protestant movement that arose in Zürich in 1525 and spread through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>. Anabaptists rejected infant baptism, baptising only adult believers, and generally sought to separate the church from the state; Catholics and Protestants alike persecuted them. After the violent episode of Münster in 1534 to 1535, the movement turned to pacifism, surviving in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mennonites">Mennonites</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutterites">Hutterites</a> and Amish.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anabaptism.jpg" alt="Anabaptism" width="320" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jan Luyken · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dirk.willems.rescue.ncs.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
